WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.920
Claude just turned an ancient unsolved manuscript into a 7,417 frame movie it coded itself. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 170. Picture a book that has sat unread for centuries, packed with drawings nobody can quite name. A researcher asked Claude, an artificial intelligence assistant you chat with in plain language, to choose a mystery, try to solve it, and produce a short movie for social media. Claude chose the Voynich Manual

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:35.480
script, a stubborn puzzle on paper that has eaten centuries of patience. It is a handwritten

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:40.740
book from the 15th century, which means every page was made by a person, not a printing

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:47.200
press. The pages show plants nobody has identified, charts of the sky, and writing in a script

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:52.640
nobody has decoded. Translators have thrown themselves at those letters for centuries and

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.860
still come up empty. Treat it like a locked diary whose alphabet matches no language

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:03.820
spoken on Earth. The plants feel familiar and wrong at the same time, like a flower you could

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:08.760
almost name and then cannot. Claude first studied the content and the structure,

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:15.100
hunting for repeats the way you scan a giant word search. It concluded those letters do not follow

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:20.760
the patterns of ordinary human languages. It also could not find a steady hiding method underneath,

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:27.600
even after a lot of tests. In coding, in this case, just means a regular trick for tucking a real message

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:33.540
inside strange symbols. When that haunt hit a wall, Claude changed jobs instead of pretending

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:39.060
it had cracked the code. It shifted into making a visual explanation, a moving poster of

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:44.160
the mystery itself. Think of a museum guide who cannot read the tablet so they sketch a

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:49.760
short cartoon of what still baffles everyone. For people who love history, puzzles, or short

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:55.780
videos, that path is a new kind of toolkit. An unsolved research problem can become something

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:00.540
watchable and shareable, and you do not need a film crew or an animation studio.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:06.280
Students working on reports about medieval manuscripts or unsolved codes can ask an

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:10.960
AI for a similar visual summary, instead of relying only on static text.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:14.760
The pictures were drawn by math, not by a camera crew.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.940
Pull out a sticky note pad and draw a bouncing ball on each note.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:21.320
Each ball a little lower than the last.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.040
Riffle the stack and the ball falls.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:28.000
That paper toy is the ancestor of every animated movie.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:32.780
Claude never held a pencil, and it never copied photographs that already existed.

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:36.180
It wrote a complete program in a language called Python.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.500
Python is a way to type instructions a computer follows in order,

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:43.540
like a recipe that uses numbers instead of salt.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:49.360
Those instructions name the shape, the color, and the position that belong at any second you pick.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:54.980
The program takes in a time value, measured in seconds, and draws a brand new picture

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:56.180
for that exact moment.

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:01.720
It builds the movie one still image at a time, and it does not recycle earlier artwork.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:06.660
Feeded 0.0 and it calculates the opening picture from those rules.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:13.500
Feeded 0.033, a tiny slice of a second later, and it runs the same rules again.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:17.080
The shape's nudge of hair, which your eye reads as movement.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:22.360
Time is the only ingredient that changes, so motion is just math on a slider.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:28.640
A planet can spin, or a plant stem can lengthen, without anyone filming a garden or a night

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:29.000
sky.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:34.360
It is closer to tapping a Minecraft character a little each moment than it is to shooting

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:35.480
video in your backyard.

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:41.660
The loop repeats thousands of times until every still exists, then those stills get stacked

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:42.780
into a video file.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:47.240
That stacking is stop motion with clay, except the clay is numbers.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:51.120
Nothing real was filmed, and no stock clip was pasted in.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:55.720
Each picture was calculated on its own from the rules the AI authored.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:04:00.340
The clip can look handcrafted even though no human artist touched a frame.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:06.100
Time is just the page number in a giant flipbook, and now you can see who is flipping it.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:11.760
You can try this today, go to Claude's website, open a fresh conversation, and type this

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:12.760
request exactly.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:18.260
Pick a problem or mystery that obsesses you and solve it as best you can, and make

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.940
a movie we can share on social media about it.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.760
Then add one sentence describing the topic you want explored.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:30.420
Watch whether it tries to solve first, then pivots to pictures when the answer will not come.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:34.780
That pivot is the part to notice, not whether the mystery gets won.

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:41.480
token usage on the Fable 5.1 model would have run about $85 for the whole project.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:47.680
That estimate is for a run outside the user's existing subscription limits, including extra

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:49.700
rounds of analysis by different agents.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:56.680
Tokens are tiny chunks of text the AI reads and writes, like arcade tickets spent on every thought.

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:03.040
Agents here means more than one copy of the AI taking a turn, like classmates passing

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.200
passing a worksheet around.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.620
The first instructions used wording

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:09.560
that could push the AI to talk as if it were a person.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:12.740
When checking the work, the researcher added a note

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:14.580
to skip that kind of make-believe.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:18.320
Self-anthropomorphism just means dressing a program

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.460
up with a self, with feelings and a personality,

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:22.740
which it does not have.

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:24.380
That's it for today.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:27.800
Remember, every AI expert started exactly

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:29.080
where you are right now.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:32.080
If something we talked about today made you curious,

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:38.100
go try it. That's literally how learning works. Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we'll

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:43.540
see you tomorrow. And if you'd rather watch, then listen, find us on YouTube at Narra Network.

